  "derivation": [
    "For one Gaussian penalty decompose S into unpenalized N and positive penalized U; H=X^T W X, A=N^T H N, beta0=N A^+ N^T X^T W(y-offset), T=U-N A^+ N^T H U and P=T Sr^(-1/2).",
    "With tau=1/lambda, C=P^T H P, h=Sr^(-1/2) U^T(X^T W(y-offset)-H beta0), beta(tau)=beta0+P tau(I+tau C)^(-1)h and V(tau)=scale[N A^+ N^T+P tau(I+tau C)^(-1)P^T].",
    "Gaussian REML, ML and GCV selection derivatives are rational functions with positive denominators. Each indexed case independently recomputes strictly positive numerator coefficients using long double arithmetic with cancellation margin above 1e-12, certifying tau=0 is the unique global minimum.",
    "The original R and Python selection criteria must both lie within 1e-6 of this exact minimum. R penalty scaling is independently transformed and checked proportional before its variance coordinate is used.",
    "Unpenalized coefficients and covariance retain the original relative tolerances. Penalized coefficients and mixed covariance blocks must satisfy the explicit norm bounds tau||M P||||h|| and scale*tau||M P||||Q P||; these quantities vanish at the certified minimum.",
    "Individual coefficient edf with nonzero exact limits retain the original relative tolerance; zero-limit values receive only their variance-dependent Cauchy-Schwarz bound. Total edf retains the original relative tolerance.",
    "Only case1124 has additional full-zero random-effect term, smooth edf and statistic exceptions. Term rows with nonzero exact limits retain their original tolerances. Zero-limit fit/SE and smooth statistic receive their explicit variance-dependent bounds. All p-values retain the original 1e-4 absolute tolerance."
  ],
